1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,060
you

2
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:32,060
you

3
00:00:36,320 --> 00:00:40,040
Krishnamurti in dialogue with Dr. Alan

4
00:00:40,040 --> 00:00:43,760
W. Anderson. J. Krishnamurti was born in

5
00:00:43,760 --> 00:00:46,360
South India and educated in England. For

6
00:00:46,360 --> 00:00:48,960
the past 40 years he has been speaking

7
00:00:48,960 --> 00:00:51,440
in the United States, Europe, India,

8
00:00:51,440 --> 00:00:53,920
Australia, and other parts of the world.

9
00:00:53,920 --> 00:00:56,320
From the outset of his life's

10
00:00:56,320 --> 00:00:58,720
work he repudiated all connections with

11
00:00:58,720 --> 00:01:01,320
organized religions and ideologies and said that

12
00:01:01,320 --> 00:01:03,920
his only concern was to set men

13
00:01:03,920 --> 00:01:07,140
absolutely unconditionally free. He is the

14
00:01:07,140 --> 00:01:10,360
author of many books, among them the

15
00:01:10,360 --> 00:01:13,380
Awakening of Intelligence, The Urgency of

16
00:01:13,380 --> 00:01:16,400
Change, Freedom from the Known, and The

17
00:01:16,400 --> 00:01:19,260
Flight of the Eagle. This is one

18
00:01:19,260 --> 00:01:22,120
of a series of dialogues between Krishnamurti

19
00:01:22,120 --> 00:01:24,240
and Dr. Alan W. Anderson, who

20
00:01:24,240 --> 00:01:26,360
is professor of religious studies at San

21
00:01:26,360 --> 00:01:28,520
Diego State University where he teaches

22
00:01:28,520 --> 00:01:30,680
Indian and Chinese scriptures and the

23
00:01:30,680 --> 00:01:33,700
oracular tradition. Dr. Anderson, a

24
00:01:33,700 --> 00:01:36,720
published poet, received his degree from

25
00:01:36,720 --> 00:01:39,040
Columbia University and the Union

26
00:01:39,040 --> 00:01:41,360
Theological Seminary. He has been honored

27
00:01:41,360 --> 00:01:44,400
with a Distinguished Teaching Award

28
00:01:44,400 --> 00:01:47,440
from the California State Universities.

29
00:01:48,000 --> 00:01:51,480
Mr. Krishnamurti, during our conversations

30
00:01:51,480 --> 00:01:54,960
one thing has emerged for

31
00:01:54,960 --> 00:01:59,240
me with, I'd say, an arresting force and

32
00:01:59,240 --> 00:02:03,520
that is, on the one hand we've been

33
00:02:03,520 --> 00:02:06,519
talking about thought and knowledge in

34
00:02:06,519 --> 00:02:09,519
terms of a dysfunctional relationship to

35
00:02:09,519 --> 00:02:11,859
it, but never once have you said

36
00:02:11,859 --> 00:02:14,200
that we should get rid of thought. You've

37
00:02:14,200 --> 00:02:17,620
never said that knowledge as such

38
00:02:17,620 --> 00:02:21,040
in itself has something profoundly the

39
00:02:21,040 --> 00:02:23,960
matter with it. Therefore

40
00:02:23,960 --> 00:02:26,880
the relationship between intelligence and

41
00:02:26,880 --> 00:02:31,839
thought arises, and the question of what

42
00:02:31,839 --> 00:02:36,799
seems to be that which maintains a

43
00:02:36,799 --> 00:02:39,659
creative relationship between intelligence

44
00:02:39,659 --> 00:02:42,519
and thought, perhaps some

45
00:02:42,600 --> 00:02:47,260
primordial activity which abides, and in

46
00:02:47,260 --> 00:02:51,920
thinking on this I wondered whether you

47
00:02:51,920 --> 00:02:57,380
would agree that perhaps in the history

48
00:02:57,380 --> 00:03:02,840
of human existence the concept of God

49
00:03:02,840 --> 00:03:08,580
has been generated out of a

50
00:03:08,580 --> 00:03:14,320
relationship to this abiding activity, which concept

51
00:03:14,320 --> 00:03:17,140
has been very badly abused, and it

52
00:03:17,140 --> 00:03:19,960
raises the whole question of the phenomenon of

53
00:03:19,960 --> 00:03:23,640
religion itself. I wondered if we might discuss that today.

54
00:03:23,640 --> 00:03:31,560
Yes. You know, the word like religion, love

55
00:03:31,560 --> 00:03:39,480
or God, has almost lost all its meaning.

56
00:03:39,480 --> 00:03:45,719
They have abused these words so

57
00:03:45,719 --> 00:03:51,959
enormously. A religion has become a vast

58
00:03:51,959 --> 00:03:56,959
superstition, a great propaganda,

59
00:03:56,959 --> 00:04:01,959
incredible beliefs and superstitions,

60
00:04:02,120 --> 00:04:07,260
worship of images made by the hand or

61
00:04:07,260 --> 00:04:12,400
by the mind. So when we talk about

62
00:04:12,400 --> 00:04:17,279
religion, I would like, if I may, to be

63
00:04:17,279 --> 00:04:22,160
quite clear that we are, both of us, are

64
00:04:22,520 --> 00:04:27,560
using the word religion in the real sense

65
00:04:27,560 --> 00:04:32,600
of that word, not in the either Christian

66
00:04:32,600 --> 00:04:35,900
or the Hindu or the Muslim or the Buddhist

67
00:04:35,900 --> 00:04:39,200
or all the stupid things that are going on

68
00:04:39,200 --> 00:04:45,080
in this country in the name of religion.

69
00:04:45,080 --> 00:04:50,960
I think the word religion means gathering together

70
00:04:50,960 --> 00:04:57,800
all energy at all levels – physical, moral,

71
00:04:57,800 --> 00:05:04,640
spiritual – at all levels, gathering all this energy

72
00:05:08,159 --> 00:05:13,999
which will bring about a great attention.

73
00:05:13,999 --> 00:05:19,840
And in that attention there is no frontier,

74
00:05:19,840 --> 00:05:27,039
and then from there move to meet – that is

75
00:05:27,039 --> 00:05:34,239
the meaning of that word – the gathering of total energy

76
00:05:34,240 --> 00:05:43,200
to understand what thought cannot possibly

77
00:05:43,200 --> 00:05:52,160
capture. Thought is never new, never free,

78
00:05:52,160 --> 00:05:55,140
and therefore it is always conditioned and

79
00:05:55,140 --> 00:05:58,120
fragmentary and so on, which we discussed.

80
00:05:58,120 --> 00:06:06,180
So religion is not a thing put together by

81
00:06:06,180 --> 00:06:14,240
thought or by fear or by the pursuit of satisfaction

82
00:06:14,240 --> 00:06:22,060
and pleasure, but something totally beyond

83
00:06:22,060 --> 00:06:29,880
all this, which isn't romanticism, speculative

84
00:06:29,880 --> 00:06:36,060
belief or sentimentality. And I think if we

85
00:06:36,060 --> 00:06:42,240
could keep to the meaning of that word, putting

86
00:06:42,240 --> 00:06:45,560
all aside all the superstitious nonsense that is

87
00:06:45,560 --> 00:06:48,880
going on in the world in the name

88
00:06:48,880 --> 00:06:52,260
of religion, which has become really quite

89
00:06:52,260 --> 00:06:55,640
a circus, however beautiful it is, then I

90
00:06:55,640 --> 00:07:01,020
think we could from there start, if you will,

91
00:07:01,020 --> 00:07:06,400
if you agree to the meaning of that word.

92
00:07:07,159 --> 00:07:11,099
I've been thinking as you've been speaking

93
00:07:11,099 --> 00:07:15,039
that in the biblical tradition there are actual

94
00:07:15,039 --> 00:07:19,919
statements from the prophets which seem to

95
00:07:19,919 --> 00:07:24,799
point to what you're saying. Such things

96
00:07:24,799 --> 00:07:28,499
come to mind as Isaiah's taking the part

97
00:07:28,499 --> 00:07:32,200
of the divine when he says, My thoughts are

98
00:07:32,200 --> 00:07:34,420
not your thoughts, my ways not your ways,

99
00:07:34,420 --> 00:07:36,640
as high as the heavens are above the earth

100
00:07:36,640 --> 00:07:39,300
so are my thoughts and your thoughts, so

101
00:07:39,300 --> 00:07:41,960
stop thinking about me in that sense, and don't

102
00:07:41,960 --> 00:07:45,080
try to find a means to me that you've

103
00:07:45,080 --> 00:07:48,200
contrived, since my ways are higher than your ways. And

104
00:07:48,200 --> 00:07:51,740
then I was thinking while you were

105
00:07:51,740 --> 00:07:55,280
speaking concerning this act of attention, this gathering

106
00:07:55,280 --> 00:08:00,499
together of all energies of the whole man,

107
00:08:00,499 --> 00:08:05,719
the very simple, be still and know that I'm

108
00:08:05,719 --> 00:08:09,279
God, be still. It's amazing when one thinks

109
00:08:09,279 --> 00:08:12,840
of the history of religion how little attention

110
00:08:12,840 --> 00:08:15,840
has been paid to that as compared with ritual.

111
00:08:15,840 --> 00:08:24,220
And I think when we lost touch with

112
00:08:24,220 --> 00:08:32,600
nature, with the universe, with the clouds, lakes,

113
00:08:32,600 --> 00:08:37,519
birds, when we lost touch with all that,

114
00:08:37,519 --> 00:08:42,439
then the priest came in, then all the superstition,

115
00:08:43,039 --> 00:08:50,220
fears, exploitation, all that began. The priest

116
00:08:50,220 --> 00:08:57,400
became the mediator between the human and

117
00:08:57,400 --> 00:09:07,920
the so-called divine. And I believe, if you have read...

118
00:09:11,074 --> 00:09:13,074
ركويتاً

119
00:09:13,538 --> 00:09:15,038
or I was told about it

120
00:09:15,038 --> 00:09:16,538
because I don't read all this,

121
00:09:16,538 --> 00:09:19,858
that there, in the first way round,

122
00:09:19,858 --> 00:09:25,338
there is no mention of God at all.

123
00:09:25,658 --> 00:09:32,698
There is only this worship of something immense,

124
00:09:32,898 --> 00:09:35,418
expressed in nature, in the earth,

125
00:09:35,418 --> 00:09:37,938
in the clouds, in the trees,

126
00:09:37,938 --> 00:09:40,818
in the beauty of vision.

127
00:09:40,818 --> 00:09:46,418
But that being very, very simple,

128
00:09:46,418 --> 00:09:51,218
the priest said, that's too simple.

129
00:09:51,218 --> 00:09:53,218
A. Let's mix it up. K. Let's mix it up,

130
00:09:53,218 --> 00:09:58,338
let's confuse it a little bit. And then began.

131
00:09:58,338 --> 00:10:01,578
I believe this is traceable

132
00:10:01,578 --> 00:10:06,218
from the ancient Vedas to present time,

133
00:10:06,258 --> 00:10:12,138
where the priest became the interpreter,

134
00:10:12,138 --> 00:10:20,218
the mediator, the explainer, the exploiter,

135
00:10:20,218 --> 00:10:23,618
the man who said, this is right, this is wrong,

136
00:10:23,618 --> 00:10:26,038
you must believe this, or you

137
00:10:26,038 --> 00:10:28,458
go to perdition, and so on.

138
00:10:28,458 --> 00:10:33,018
He generated fear.

139
00:10:34,018 --> 00:10:39,418
Not the adoration of beauty,

140
00:10:39,418 --> 00:10:45,098
not the adoration of life

141
00:10:45,098 --> 00:10:50,618
lived totally, wholly without conflict,

142
00:10:51,418 --> 00:10:55,578
but something placed outside there,

143
00:10:55,578 --> 00:11:02,378
beyond and above what he considered to be God,

144
00:11:02,378 --> 00:11:07,058
and made propaganda for that.

145
00:11:07,058 --> 00:11:13,658
So, I feel if we could, from the beginning,

146
00:11:14,898 --> 00:11:21,538
use the word religion in the simplest way,

147
00:11:21,538 --> 00:11:24,778
that is, the gathering of all energy

148
00:11:24,778 --> 00:11:28,978
so that there is total attention,

149
00:11:29,258 --> 00:11:32,897
and in that quality of attention

150
00:11:32,897 --> 00:11:37,218
the immeasurable comes into being.

151
00:11:39,938 --> 00:11:42,498
Because, as we said the other day,

152
00:11:42,498 --> 00:11:47,378
the measurable is the mechanical,

153
00:11:49,858 --> 00:11:55,378
which the West has cultivated,

154
00:11:55,418 --> 00:12:00,578
made marvellous, technologically, physically,

155
00:12:00,578 --> 00:12:05,778
medicine, science, biology, and so on,

156
00:12:05,778 --> 00:12:10,178
which has made the world so superficial,

157
00:12:10,178 --> 00:12:15,258
mechanical, worldly, materialistic,

158
00:12:15,258 --> 00:12:19,938
and that is spreading all over the world.

159
00:12:20,698 --> 00:12:25,658
And in reaction to that,

160
00:12:27,178 --> 00:12:30,458
this materialistic attitude,

161
00:12:30,458 --> 00:12:35,458
there are all these superstitious, nonsensical,

162
00:12:35,458 --> 00:12:41,458
unreasoned religion that is going on.

163
00:12:41,458 --> 00:12:43,738
I don't know if you saw the other day

164
00:12:43,738 --> 00:12:47,858
the absurdity of these gurus coming from India

165
00:12:47,858 --> 00:12:50,418
and teaching the West how to meditate,

166
00:12:50,418 --> 00:12:52,617
how to hold breath, how they say,

167
00:12:52,617 --> 00:12:56,378
I am God, worship me, and falling there.

168
00:12:56,378 --> 00:13:00,858
It has become so absurd,

169
00:13:02,098 --> 00:13:06,818
and childish, so utterly immature.

170
00:13:06,818 --> 00:13:13,858
All that indicates the degradation of the word religion,

171
00:13:14,018 --> 00:13:16,497
and the human mind that can accept

172
00:13:16,497 --> 00:13:20,818
this kind of circus and idiocy.

173
00:13:22,617 --> 00:13:24,617
Now, please, sir.

174
00:13:24,617 --> 00:13:26,857
A.S. – I was thinking

175
00:13:26,857 --> 00:13:29,098
of a remark of Sri Aurobindo's

176
00:13:29,098 --> 00:13:32,738
in a study that he made of the Veda,

177
00:13:32,738 --> 00:13:36,577
where he traced its decline in the sentence.

178
00:13:36,577 --> 00:13:42,098
He said it issues as language from sages,

179
00:13:42,138 --> 00:13:44,338
then it falls to the priests,

180
00:13:44,338 --> 00:13:46,938
and then after the priests it falls to the scholars

181
00:13:46,938 --> 00:13:48,658
or the academicians.

182
00:13:48,658 --> 00:13:51,158
But in that study there

183
00:13:51,158 --> 00:13:53,658
was no statement that I found

184
00:13:53,658 --> 00:13:58,098
as to how it ever fell to the priests.

185
00:13:58,098 --> 00:14:00,178
And I was wondering whether...

186
00:14:00,178 --> 00:14:02,098
K.P. – Isn't this fairly simple, sir?

187
00:14:02,098 --> 00:14:03,738
A.S. – Yes, please.

188
00:14:03,738 --> 00:14:05,338
K.P. – Isn't this fairly simple,

189
00:14:05,338 --> 00:14:10,138
how the priests got hold of the whole business?

190
00:14:10,138 --> 00:14:13,138
Because a man

191
00:14:14,898 --> 00:14:19,898
is so concerned with his own petty little affairs,

192
00:14:19,898 --> 00:14:23,818
petty little desires and ambitions, superficiality,

193
00:14:23,818 --> 00:14:27,458
he wants something a little more.

194
00:14:28,218 --> 00:14:34,818
He wants a little more romantic, a little more sentimental,

195
00:14:34,858 --> 00:14:40,538
something other than the daily, beastly routine of living.

196
00:14:40,538 --> 00:14:42,818
So he looks somewhere and the priest says,

197
00:14:42,818 --> 00:14:46,777
hey, come over here, I've got the goods.

198
00:14:46,777 --> 00:14:50,257
I think it's very simple

199
00:14:50,257 --> 00:14:53,738
how the priests have come in.

200
00:14:53,738 --> 00:14:55,838
You see it in India,

201
00:14:55,838 --> 00:14:57,938
you see it in the West,

202
00:14:57,938 --> 00:15:04,418
you see it everywhere where man begins to...

203
00:15:04,498 --> 00:15:09,018
be concerned with daily living,

204
00:15:09,018 --> 00:15:12,858
the daily operation of bread and butter and house

205
00:15:12,858 --> 00:15:14,178
and all the rest of it.

206
00:15:14,178 --> 00:15:18,418
He demands something more than that.

207
00:15:19,538 --> 00:15:21,058
He says, after all, I'll die,

208
00:15:21,058 --> 00:15:24,818
but there must be something more.

209
00:15:25,098 --> 00:15:27,578
A.S. – So fundamentally it's

210
00:15:27,578 --> 00:15:30,058
a matter of securing for himself

211
00:15:30,058 --> 00:15:33,058
some heavenly grace...

212
00:15:33,058 --> 00:15:35,577
A.S. – ...some heavenly grace that will preserve him

213
00:15:35,577 --> 00:15:39,498
against falling into this mournful round

214
00:15:39,498 --> 00:15:42,257
of coming to be and passing away.

215
00:15:42,257 --> 00:15:45,137
Thinking of the past on the one hand,

216
00:15:45,137 --> 00:15:47,658
anticipating the future on the other,

217
00:15:47,658 --> 00:15:50,418
you're saying he falls out of the present now.

218
00:15:50,418 --> 00:15:54,058
K. Yes, that's right. A.S. – I understand.

219
00:15:54,058 --> 00:15:57,757
K. So if we could keep

220
00:15:57,757 --> 00:16:01,457
to that meaning of that word, religion,

221
00:16:01,458 --> 00:16:07,058
then from there the question arises,

222
00:16:07,058 --> 00:16:18,018
can the mind be so attentive in the total sense

223
00:16:18,018 --> 00:16:23,498
that the unnameable comes into being?

224
00:16:24,498 --> 00:16:29,298
You see, personally,

225
00:16:29,298 --> 00:16:31,017
I've never read any of these things,

226
00:16:31,017 --> 00:16:35,897
Vedas, Bhagavad Gita, Upanishads, all the rest of it,

227
00:16:35,897 --> 00:16:39,658
or any philosophy,

228
00:16:39,658 --> 00:16:43,697
but I questioned everything.

229
00:16:43,697 --> 00:16:46,298
A.S. – Yes.

230
00:16:46,298 --> 00:16:50,697
K. Not questioned only, but observed.

231
00:16:51,498 --> 00:16:58,498
And one sees the absolute necessity

232
00:16:58,498 --> 00:17:03,098
of a mind that's completely quiet.

233
00:17:07,578 --> 00:17:10,377
Because it's only out of quietness

234
00:17:10,377 --> 00:17:15,098
you perceive what is happening.

235
00:17:15,778 --> 00:17:22,098
If I am chattering, I won't listen to you.

236
00:17:22,098 --> 00:17:25,498
If my mind is constantly rattling away,

237
00:17:25,498 --> 00:17:29,298
what you are saying, I won't pay attention.

238
00:17:29,298 --> 00:17:33,498
To pay attention means to be quiet.

239
00:17:35,618 --> 00:17:39,258
A.S. – There have been some priests, apparently,

240
00:17:39,258 --> 00:17:40,898
who usually ended up in a

241
00:17:40,898 --> 00:17:42,538
great deal of trouble for it,

242
00:17:42,618 --> 00:17:46,538
there have been some priests who had,

243
00:17:46,538 --> 00:17:48,138
it seems, a grasp of this.

244
00:17:48,138 --> 00:17:52,538
I was thinking of Meister Eckhart's remark that

245
00:17:52,538 --> 00:17:55,658
whoever is able to read the book of nature

246
00:17:55,658 --> 00:17:57,138
doesn't need any scriptures at all.

247
00:17:57,138 --> 00:17:58,738
K. At all. That's just it.

248
00:17:58,738 --> 00:18:03,458
A.S. Of course, he ended up in great trouble.

249
00:18:03,458 --> 00:18:04,658
Yes, he had a bad time

250
00:18:04,658 --> 00:18:05,858
toward the end of his life,

251
00:18:05,858 --> 00:18:09,138
and after he died the church denounced him.

252
00:18:09,138 --> 00:18:11,338
Of course, of course.

253
00:18:11,338 --> 00:18:15,538
Organised belief as church and all the rest of it

254
00:18:15,538 --> 00:18:19,658
is too obvious.

255
00:18:19,658 --> 00:18:22,358
It isn't subtle, it hasn't

256
00:18:22,358 --> 00:18:25,058
got the quality of real depth

257
00:18:25,058 --> 00:18:27,698
and real spirituality. You know what it is.

258
00:18:27,698 --> 00:18:29,018
A.S. Yes, I do.

259
00:18:29,018 --> 00:18:33,538
K. So, I'm asking,

260
00:18:33,538 --> 00:18:37,018
what is the quality of a mind,

261
00:18:37,018 --> 00:18:39,258
and therefore heart and brain,

262
00:18:39,258 --> 00:18:41,618
what is the quality of a mind

263
00:18:41,618 --> 00:18:47,978
that can perceive something

264
00:18:47,978 --> 00:18:52,458
beyond the measurement of thought?

265
00:18:56,338 --> 00:18:59,658
What is the quality of a mind?

266
00:18:59,658 --> 00:19:04,458
Because that quality is the religious mind.

267
00:19:05,458 --> 00:19:11,977
That quality of a mind that is capable,

268
00:19:11,977 --> 00:19:18,458
that has this feeling of being sacred in itself,

269
00:19:18,458 --> 00:19:21,818
and therefore is capable of seeing something

270
00:19:21,818 --> 00:19:25,778
immeasurably sacred.

271
00:19:27,658 --> 00:19:30,338
A.S. The word devotion seems to imply this

272
00:19:30,338 --> 00:19:34,338
when it's grasped in its proper sense.

273
00:19:34,578 --> 00:19:36,497
To use your earlier phrase,

274
00:19:36,497 --> 00:19:46,377
gathering together toward a one-pointed attentive.

275
00:19:46,377 --> 00:19:51,098
K. Would you say attention is one-pointed?

276
00:19:51,098 --> 00:19:53,357
A.S. No, I didn't mean to

277
00:19:53,357 --> 00:19:55,617
imply focus when I said one-pointed.

278
00:19:55,617 --> 00:20:00,977
I meant rather integrated into itself

279
00:20:01,018 --> 00:20:07,938
as utterly quiet and unconcerned

280
00:20:07,938 --> 00:20:12,058
about taking thought for what

281
00:20:12,058 --> 00:20:16,178
is ahead or what is behind.

282
00:20:16,178 --> 00:20:19,898
Simply being there.

283
00:20:19,898 --> 00:20:21,618
The word there isn't good either,

284
00:20:21,618 --> 00:20:24,458
because it suggests that there's a where,

285
00:20:24,458 --> 00:20:28,738
and a here, and all the rest of it.

286
00:20:28,778 --> 00:20:31,178
It is very difficult to

287
00:20:31,178 --> 00:20:33,578
find, it seems to me, language,

288
00:20:33,578 --> 00:20:37,178
to do justice to what you're saying,

289
00:20:37,178 --> 00:20:40,338
precisely because when we speak,

290
00:20:40,338 --> 00:20:44,418
utterance is in time, and it is progressive.

291
00:20:44,418 --> 00:20:46,378
It has a quality, doesn't it?

292
00:20:46,378 --> 00:20:49,898
More like music than we see in graphic art.

293
00:20:49,898 --> 00:20:52,498
You can stand before a picture,

294
00:20:52,498 --> 00:20:56,618
whereas to hear music and grasp its theme,

295
00:20:56,658 --> 00:20:57,978
you virtually have to wait

296
00:20:57,978 --> 00:20:59,298
until you get to the end

297
00:20:59,298 --> 00:21:01,378
and gather it all up.

298
00:21:01,378 --> 00:21:06,018
And with language you have the same difficulty.

299
00:21:06,018 --> 00:21:10,178
K. No, I think, sir, wouldn't you,

300
00:21:10,178 --> 00:21:14,938
when we are enquiring into this problem,

301
00:21:14,938 --> 00:21:22,338
what is the nature and the structure of a mind,

302
00:21:23,338 --> 00:21:27,698
and therefore the quality of a mind

303
00:21:27,698 --> 00:21:33,257
that is not only sacred and holy in itself,

304
00:21:33,257 --> 00:21:40,578
but is capable of seeing something immense.

305
00:21:43,458 --> 00:21:48,458
As we were talking the other day about suffering,

306
00:21:49,458 --> 00:21:53,538
the personal and the sorrow of the world,

307
00:21:53,538 --> 00:21:59,737
it isn't that we must suffer.

308
00:21:59,737 --> 00:22:03,058
Suffering is there.

309
00:22:03,058 --> 00:22:07,698
Every human being has a dreadful time with it,

310
00:22:07,698 --> 00:22:10,578
and there is suffering of the world.

311
00:22:10,578 --> 00:22:14,698
And it isn't that one must go through it,

312
00:22:15,257 --> 00:22:18,317
but as it is there, one

313
00:22:18,317 --> 00:22:21,377
must understand it and go beyond it.

314
00:22:21,377 --> 00:22:26,778
And that's one of the qualities of a religious mind,

315
00:22:26,778 --> 00:22:29,857
in the sense we are using that word,

316
00:22:29,857 --> 00:22:35,778
that is incapable of suffering.

317
00:22:37,338 --> 00:22:40,698
It has gone beyond it,

318
00:22:40,698 --> 00:22:47,098
which doesn't mean that it becomes callous.

319
00:22:47,098 --> 00:22:52,898
On the contrary, it is a passionate mind.

320
00:22:57,257 --> 00:22:59,497
A. One of the things

321
00:22:59,497 --> 00:23:01,737
that I have thought much about

322
00:23:01,737 --> 00:23:06,258
during our conversations is language itself.

323
00:23:06,418 --> 00:23:08,458
On the one hand we say,

324
00:23:08,458 --> 00:23:12,538
such a mind, as you have been describing,

325
00:23:12,538 --> 00:23:18,498
is one that is present to suffering.

326
00:23:18,498 --> 00:23:22,818
It does nothing to push

327
00:23:22,818 --> 00:23:27,138
it away, on the one hand.

328
00:23:27,202 --> 00:23:28,202
و

329
00:23:28,202 --> 00:23:29,202
لكن

330
00:23:29,410 --> 00:23:34,970
it is somehow able to contain it,

331
00:23:34,990 --> 00:23:38,050
not put it in a vase or a barrel

332
00:23:38,070 --> 00:23:41,650
and contain it in that sense.

333
00:23:41,670 --> 00:23:45,650
And yet the very word itself, to suffer,

334
00:23:45,670 --> 00:23:49,290
means to under-carry,

335
00:23:49,310 --> 00:23:53,050
and it seems close to understand.

336
00:23:53,070 --> 00:23:55,050
Over and over again in our conversations

337
00:23:55,050 --> 00:24:01,450
I've been thinking about the customary way

338
00:24:01,470 --> 00:24:05,320
in which we use language

339
00:24:05,320 --> 00:24:09,170
as a use that deprives us

340
00:24:09,190 --> 00:24:12,130
of really seeing the glory

341
00:24:12,150 --> 00:24:17,850
of what the word points to itself in itself.

342
00:24:17,870 --> 00:24:19,730
I was thinking about the word religion

343
00:24:19,750 --> 00:24:23,050
when we were speaking earlier.

344
00:24:23,090 --> 00:24:25,610
Scholars differ as to where that came from.

345
00:24:25,630 --> 00:24:27,380
On the one hand some

346
00:24:27,380 --> 00:24:29,130
say that it means to bind.

347
00:24:29,150 --> 00:24:31,210
K. Biner, really a guarantee.

348
00:24:31,230 --> 00:24:32,730
A. They spoke about that.

349
00:24:32,750 --> 00:24:37,490
And then others say, no, no, it means the numinous,

350
00:24:37,510 --> 00:24:45,970
or the splendour that cannot be exhausted by thought.

351
00:24:45,990 --> 00:24:51,490
It seems to me that, wouldn't you say,

352
00:24:51,530 --> 00:24:53,950
there is another sense to bind

353
00:24:53,950 --> 00:24:56,370
that is not a negative one,

354
00:24:56,390 --> 00:25:00,100
in the sense that if one

355
00:25:00,100 --> 00:25:03,810
is making this act of attention,

356
00:25:03,810 --> 00:25:06,770
one isn't bound as with cords of ropes,

357
00:25:06,790 --> 00:25:12,290
but one is there, or here.

358
00:25:12,310 --> 00:25:15,010
K. Now again, let's be clear.

359
00:25:15,030 --> 00:25:20,090
When we use the word attention,

360
00:25:20,090 --> 00:25:25,090
there is a difference between concentration and attention.

361
00:25:25,090 --> 00:25:30,570
Concentration is exclusion.

362
00:25:30,570 --> 00:25:34,730
I concentrate.

363
00:25:34,730 --> 00:25:41,170
That is, bring all my thinking to a certain point.

364
00:25:41,170 --> 00:25:47,770
And therefore it is excluding,

365
00:25:47,790 --> 00:25:49,990
building a barrier

366
00:25:49,990 --> 00:25:54,670
so that it can focus its whole attention on that,

367
00:25:54,670 --> 00:25:56,950
whole concentration on that.

368
00:25:56,950 --> 00:26:00,330
Whereas attention is something

369
00:26:00,330 --> 00:26:03,710
entirely different from concentration.

370
00:26:03,710 --> 00:26:06,710
In that there is no exclusion,

371
00:26:06,710 --> 00:26:08,870
in that there is no resistance,

372
00:26:08,870 --> 00:26:12,670
in that there is no effort,

373
00:26:12,730 --> 00:26:18,290
and therefore no frontier, no limits.

374
00:26:22,929 --> 00:26:25,309
A. How would you feel about

375
00:26:25,309 --> 00:26:27,690
the word receptive in this respect?

376
00:26:27,690 --> 00:26:32,890
K. Again, who is it that is to receive?

377
00:26:35,290 --> 00:26:40,530
A. Already we have made a division with that word.

378
00:26:40,550 --> 00:26:43,150
I think the word attention

379
00:26:43,150 --> 00:26:45,750
is a really very good word,

380
00:26:46,190 --> 00:26:51,350
because it not only understands concentration,

381
00:26:51,350 --> 00:26:55,390
not only sees the duality of reception,

382
00:26:55,390 --> 00:26:58,550
the receiver and the received,

383
00:26:58,550 --> 00:27:02,710
and also it sees the nature of duality

384
00:27:02,710 --> 00:27:06,630
and the conflict of the opposites.

385
00:27:07,290 --> 00:27:10,730
And attention means

386
00:27:10,730 --> 00:27:16,650
not only the brain giving its energy,

387
00:27:16,650 --> 00:27:19,489
but also the mind, the heart, the nerve,

388
00:27:19,489 --> 00:27:23,250
the total entity, the total human mind,

389
00:27:23,250 --> 00:27:29,290
giving all its energy to perceive.

390
00:27:29,290 --> 00:27:32,630
I think that is the meaning

391
00:27:32,630 --> 00:27:35,970
of that word for me, at least.

392
00:27:35,990 --> 00:27:40,590
To be attentive, attend.

393
00:27:40,590 --> 00:27:44,950
Not concentrate, attend.

394
00:27:44,950 --> 00:27:52,710
That means listen, see, give your heart to it,

395
00:27:52,710 --> 00:27:54,550
give your mind to it,

396
00:27:54,550 --> 00:27:59,310
give your whole being to it, to attend.

397
00:27:59,310 --> 00:28:01,830
Otherwise you can't attend.

398
00:28:01,830 --> 00:28:05,630
If I am thinking about something else, I can't attend.

399
00:28:06,290 --> 00:28:12,490
If I am hearing my own voice, I can't attend.

400
00:28:13,850 --> 00:28:16,590
A. There is a metaphorical use

401
00:28:16,590 --> 00:28:19,330
of the word waiting in scripture.

402
00:28:19,330 --> 00:28:20,930
It is interesting that in English too

403
00:28:20,930 --> 00:28:26,930
we use the word attendant

404
00:28:26,930 --> 00:28:30,490
in terms of one who waits on.

405
00:28:30,509 --> 00:28:38,030
I am trying to penetrate the notion of waiting

406
00:28:38,949 --> 00:28:41,590
and patience in relation to this.

407
00:28:41,590 --> 00:28:46,230
K. Sir, waiting again means

408
00:28:46,230 --> 00:28:50,870
one who is waiting for something.

409
00:28:50,870 --> 00:28:54,030
Again there is a duality in that.

410
00:28:54,050 --> 00:29:00,050
And when you wait, you are expecting.

411
00:29:02,090 --> 00:29:04,890
Again a duality.

412
00:29:04,890 --> 00:29:10,690
One who is waiting, about to receive.

413
00:29:11,210 --> 00:29:17,090
So, if we could for the moment

414
00:29:17,090 --> 00:29:22,210
hold ourselves to that word, attention,

415
00:29:22,230 --> 00:29:26,670
then we should enquire

416
00:29:26,670 --> 00:29:29,530
what is the quality of

417
00:29:29,530 --> 00:29:32,390
a mind that is so attentive

418
00:29:32,390 --> 00:29:41,630
that it has understood, lives, acts

419
00:29:41,630 --> 00:29:47,190
in relationship and responsibility as behaviour,

420
00:29:47,190 --> 00:29:54,569
and has no fear psychologically in that we talked about,

421
00:29:54,569 --> 00:29:59,209
and therefore understands the movement of pleasure,

422
00:29:59,209 --> 00:30:01,969
then we come to the

423
00:30:01,969 --> 00:30:04,730
point, what is such a mind?

424
00:30:04,730 --> 00:30:09,610
I think it would be worthwhile if we could discuss

425
00:30:09,610 --> 00:30:13,410
the nature of hurt.

426
00:30:13,410 --> 00:30:15,850
A. Of hurt. Yes.

427
00:30:15,870 --> 00:30:21,070
Why human beings are hurt.

428
00:30:22,270 --> 00:30:25,670
All people are hurt.

429
00:30:25,670 --> 00:30:27,750
A. Both the physical and the psychological.

430
00:30:27,750 --> 00:30:29,510
K. Psychological, especially.

431
00:30:29,510 --> 00:30:31,670
A. Especially the psychological.

432
00:30:31,670 --> 00:30:34,670
K. Physically we can tolerate it,

433
00:30:34,670 --> 00:30:36,950
we can bear up with the pain and say,

434
00:30:36,950 --> 00:30:40,670
well, I won't let it interfere with my thinking,

435
00:30:40,690 --> 00:30:46,970
I won't let it corrode my psychological quality of mind.

436
00:30:46,970 --> 00:30:48,370
I can watch over it,

437
00:30:48,370 --> 00:30:49,770
the mind can watch over that.

438
00:30:49,770 --> 00:30:53,770
But the psychological hurts are much more important

439
00:30:53,770 --> 00:30:57,650
and difficult to grapple with and understand.

440
00:30:57,650 --> 00:31:01,730
I think it is necessary because

441
00:31:01,730 --> 00:31:07,410
a mind that is hurt is not an innocent mind.

442
00:31:07,410 --> 00:31:12,110
The very word in innocent comes from Noir, chérez,

443
00:31:12,110 --> 00:31:14,670
not to hurt, a mind

444
00:31:14,670 --> 00:31:17,230
that is incapable of being hurt.

445
00:31:17,230 --> 00:31:19,430
And there is a great beauty in that.

446
00:31:19,430 --> 00:31:21,790
A. Yes, there is.

447
00:31:21,790 --> 00:31:26,150
It's a marvellous word.

448
00:31:26,150 --> 00:31:28,150
We have usually used it

449
00:31:28,150 --> 00:31:30,150
to indicate a lack of something.

450
00:31:30,150 --> 00:31:33,150
Yes, and there it was turned upside down again.

451
00:31:33,650 --> 00:31:36,250
A. Yes, and there it was turned upside down again.

452
00:31:36,250 --> 00:31:38,050
K. And the Christians have made

453
00:31:38,050 --> 00:31:39,850
it such an absurd thing of it.

454
00:31:39,850 --> 00:31:42,450
A. Yes, I understand that.

455
00:31:42,450 --> 00:31:48,010
K. So, I think we ought to, when discussing religion,

456
00:31:48,010 --> 00:31:50,930
we ought to enquire very,

457
00:31:50,930 --> 00:31:53,850
very deeply the nature of hurt.

458
00:31:53,850 --> 00:31:56,690
Because if a mind that is

459
00:31:56,690 --> 00:31:59,530
not hurt is an innocent mind,

460
00:31:59,530 --> 00:32:04,430
and you need this quality of innocency,

461
00:32:06,150 --> 00:32:10,829
to be so totally attentive.

462
00:32:15,750 --> 00:32:18,110
A. If I have been

463
00:32:18,110 --> 00:32:20,470
following you correctly, I think maybe

464
00:32:20,470 --> 00:32:24,830
you would say, wouldn't you, that one becomes hurt

465
00:32:24,830 --> 00:32:30,229
when he starts thinking about thinking that he is hurt.

466
00:32:30,229 --> 00:32:34,229
K. Look, sir, it's much deeper than that, isn't it?

467
00:32:34,229 --> 00:32:37,509
From childhood,

468
00:32:38,670 --> 00:32:44,710
the parents compare the child with another child.

469
00:32:44,710 --> 00:32:47,030
A. But that's when that thought arises.

470
00:32:47,030 --> 00:32:51,430
K. There it is. When you compare, you are hurting.

471
00:32:51,430 --> 00:32:52,390
A. Yes.

472
00:32:52,390 --> 00:32:55,150
K. No, we say, but we do it.

473
00:32:55,150 --> 00:32:57,110
A. Oh, yes, of course we do it.

474
00:32:57,110 --> 00:33:00,770
K. Therefore, is it possible

475
00:33:00,770 --> 00:33:04,430
to educate a child without comparison,

476
00:33:04,830 --> 00:33:07,830
without imitation,

477
00:33:08,310 --> 00:33:15,190
and therefore never get hurt in that way?

478
00:33:15,670 --> 00:33:18,650
And one is hurt because one

479
00:33:18,650 --> 00:33:21,630
has built an image about oneself.

480
00:33:21,790 --> 00:33:25,050
The image which has built about

481
00:33:25,050 --> 00:33:28,310
oneself is a form of resistance,

482
00:33:28,870 --> 00:33:32,910
a wall between you and me.

483
00:33:32,910 --> 00:33:36,470
And when you touch that wall

484
00:33:36,470 --> 00:33:40,030
at its tender point, I get hurt.

485
00:33:40,870 --> 00:33:47,230
So, not to compare in education,

486
00:33:47,670 --> 00:33:53,470
not to have an image about oneself.

487
00:33:53,710 --> 00:33:58,150
That's one of the most important things in life,

488
00:33:58,150 --> 00:34:01,550
not to have an image about oneself.

489
00:34:01,550 --> 00:34:06,790
If you have, you are inevitably going to be hurt.

490
00:34:08,350 --> 00:34:13,110
Suppose one has an image that one is very good,

491
00:34:13,110 --> 00:34:15,870
or that one should be a great success,

492
00:34:15,870 --> 00:34:19,550
or that one has great capacities, gifts,

493
00:34:19,550 --> 00:34:22,110
and the images that one builds.

494
00:34:22,110 --> 00:34:26,469
Inevitably, you are going to come and prick it.

495
00:34:26,750 --> 00:34:31,030
Inevitably, accidents and incidents happen,

496
00:34:31,030 --> 00:34:35,870
that's going to break that, and one gets hurt.

497
00:34:36,790 --> 00:34:39,070
A. Doesn't this raise the question

498
00:34:39,070 --> 00:34:41,350
of name, the use of name?

499
00:34:41,350 --> 00:34:44,510
K. Name, form.

500
00:34:44,590 --> 00:34:46,389
The child is given a name,

501
00:34:46,389 --> 00:34:48,830
the child identifies himself with a name.

502
00:34:48,830 --> 00:34:52,550
K. Yes, the child can identify itself,

503
00:34:52,550 --> 00:34:56,830
but without the image, just a name.

504
00:34:57,070 --> 00:35:01,590
Brown, Mr Brown. There is nothing to it.

505
00:35:01,590 --> 00:35:04,550
But the moment he builds an image,

506
00:35:04,550 --> 00:35:08,590
that Mr Brown is socially,

507
00:35:08,590 --> 00:35:12,630
morally different, superior, or inferior,

508
00:35:12,630 --> 00:35:17,190
ancient, or comes from a very old family,

509
00:35:17,190 --> 00:35:23,270
belongs to a certain higher class, aristocracy,

510
00:35:23,270 --> 00:35:25,150
the moment that begins,

511
00:35:25,150 --> 00:35:30,270
and when that is encouraged and sustained by thought,

512
00:35:30,270 --> 00:35:33,350
snobbism, you know, the whole of it,

513
00:35:33,350 --> 00:35:37,750
then you are inevitably going to be hurt.

514
00:35:38,510 --> 00:35:40,470
A. Then what you are saying, I take it,

515
00:35:40,470 --> 00:35:43,470
is that the radical confusion here

516
00:35:43,470 --> 00:35:51,990
is involved in imagining oneself to be his name.

517
00:35:51,990 --> 00:35:55,270
K. Yes, identification with the name, with the body,

518
00:35:55,270 --> 00:35:59,550
with the idea of socially different,

519
00:36:00,190 --> 00:36:02,150
that your parents, your grandparents

520
00:36:02,150 --> 00:36:04,110
were lords, or this or that,

521
00:36:04,150 --> 00:36:10,190
you know, the whole snobbism of England and all that,

522
00:36:10,190 --> 00:36:14,510
and different kind of snobbism in this country.

523
00:36:14,510 --> 00:36:17,990
A. We speak in the language of preserving a name.

524
00:36:17,990 --> 00:36:20,950
K. And in India it is the Brahmin, the non-Brahmin,

525
00:36:20,950 --> 00:36:23,710
the whole business of that.

526
00:36:23,710 --> 00:36:27,450
So, through education,

527
00:36:27,450 --> 00:36:31,190
through tradition, through propaganda,

528
00:36:31,229 --> 00:36:35,349
we have built an image about ourselves.

529
00:36:36,270 --> 00:36:39,190
A. Is there a relation here in terms of religion,

530
00:36:39,190 --> 00:36:44,869
would you say, to the refusal, for instance,

531
00:36:44,869 --> 00:36:47,950
in the Hebraic tradition, to pronounce the name of God?

532
00:36:47,950 --> 00:36:50,310
K. The name.

533
00:36:50,310 --> 00:36:53,030
The word is not the thing, anyhow.

534
00:36:53,030 --> 00:36:55,470
So you can pronounce it, not pronounce it.

535
00:36:55,470 --> 00:37:00,229
No, if you know the word is never the thing.

536
00:37:00,270 --> 00:37:02,790
The description is never the

